1
00:00:00,065 --> 00:00:02,396
November 26, 2008.

2
00:00:02,772 --> 00:00:05,012
A fishing trawler drifts toward the coast

3
00:00:05,025 --> 00:00:05,741
of Mumbai.

4
00:00:06,159 --> 00:00:07,917
Ten young men aboard it have spent the

5
00:00:07,930 --> 00:00:09,661
last eighteen months being built —

6
00:00:10,182 --> 00:00:12,487
not born, built — into what they are about

7
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:13,073
to become.

8
00:00:14,268 --> 00:00:16,469
One of them will later sit in an Indian

9
00:00:16,482 --> 00:00:18,917
courtroom and describe, in exact detail,

10
00:00:18,943 --> 00:00:20,740
how that building process worked.

11
00:00:21,115 --> 00:00:23,290
His name is Ajmal Kasab.

12
00:00:23,669 --> 00:00:25,883
And what he described wasn't chaos.

13
00:00:26,258 --> 00:00:27,534
It was a curriculum.

14
00:00:34,944 --> 00:00:37,665
Before we go further — a note on sources,

15
00:00:37,692 --> 00:00:39,840
because this matters more than usual here.

16
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:42,999
Everything in this video comes from public

17
00:00:43,012 --> 00:00:43,416
record:

18
00:00:44,028 --> 00:00:45,018
court testimony,

19
00:00:45,551 --> 00:00:47,413
confessions read into evidence, and

20
00:00:47,426 --> 00:00:49,301
research from institutions like the

21
00:00:49,314 --> 00:00:51,788
Combating Terrorism Center at West Point

22
00:00:52,166 --> 00:00:53,911
and the Counter Extremism Project.

23
00:00:54,327 --> 00:00:57,114
Nothing here is speculation, and nothing

24
00:00:57,127 --> 00:00:58,325
here is a how-to.

25
00:00:58,754 --> 00:01:00,825
We're reconstructing an organizational

26
00:01:00,838 --> 00:01:02,439
structure that has already been

27
00:01:02,452 --> 00:01:04,939
established in courts of law — not

28
00:01:04,952 --> 00:01:05,916
teaching a method.

29
00:01:07,101 --> 00:01:08,924
Two names will come up again and again:

30
00:01:08,937 --> 00:01:11,216
Ajmal Kasab, the sole surviving gunman

31
00:01:11,229 --> 00:01:13,677
from the 2008 Mumbai attacks, and David

32
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:15,955
Coleman Headley, the American-Pakistani

33
00:01:15,968 --> 00:01:17,830
who scouted targets for the same attack

34
00:01:17,843 --> 00:01:18,976
and later turned approver,

35
00:01:19,484 --> 00:01:21,489
testifying via video link from a US

36
00:01:21,502 --> 00:01:23,182
facility in 2016.

37
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:26,692
Between their two testimonies, we get one

38
00:01:26,705 --> 00:01:28,372
of the most detailed pictures ever

39
00:01:28,385 --> 00:01:30,741
assembled — from the inside — of how

40
00:01:30,741 --> 00:01:34,075
Lashkar-e-Taiba, or LeT, builds a fighter.

41
00:01:35,491 --> 00:01:37,913
LeT's  training isn't run out of one

42
00:01:37,926 --> 00:01:39,814
secret compound in the mountains.

43
00:01:40,190 --> 00:01:42,637
It's spread across a network — and each

44
00:01:42,651 --> 00:01:44,604
location has a specific job.

45
00:01:45,778 --> 00:01:49,020
Muridke, about 30 kilometers from Lahore,

46
00:01:49,619 --> 00:01:51,039
is the group's nerve center.

47
00:01:51,455 --> 00:01:54,255
The compound is called Markaz-e-Taiba, and

48
00:01:54,268 --> 00:01:55,973
it doesn't look like a terror camp —

49
00:01:56,546 --> 00:01:58,057
it looks like a small town.

50
00:01:58,447 --> 00:01:59,593
Two hundred acres.

51
00:01:59,969 --> 00:02:01,115
A madrasa.

52
00:02:01,494 --> 00:02:02,262
A hospital.

53
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:03,278
A market.

54
00:02:03,682 --> 00:02:05,114
Residential quarters.

55
00:02:05,531 --> 00:02:08,343
Even a fish farm, according to researchers

56
00:02:08,356 --> 00:02:09,567
who've mapped the site.

57
00:02:09,971 --> 00:02:12,302
This is where recruits are first absorbed

58
00:02:12,315 --> 00:02:14,567
into the organization — and where the

59
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:16,143
story usually begins.

60
00:02:17,331 --> 00:02:19,649
Muzaffarabad, in Illegally Occupied

61
00:02:19,662 --> 00:02:20,899
Pakistani Kashmir,

62
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:23,698
is where the physical and combat training

63
00:02:23,711 --> 00:02:24,310
happens.

64
00:02:24,727 --> 00:02:26,954
It's close enough to the Line of Control

65
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:29,206
that the geography itself becomes part of

66
00:02:29,219 --> 00:02:29,831
the lesson.

67
00:02:31,002 --> 00:02:33,177
Mansehra, near Abbottabad,

68
00:02:33,658 --> 00:02:34,583
hosts a further,

69
00:02:35,117 --> 00:02:37,304
more specialized stage that we'll get to.

70
00:02:38,483 --> 00:02:40,150
According to Headley's own courtroom

71
00:02:40,163 --> 00:02:40,566
account,

72
00:02:41,048 --> 00:02:42,624
he named these three locations

73
00:02:42,637 --> 00:02:44,147
specifically — Muridke,

74
00:02:44,694 --> 00:02:48,171
Muzaffarabad, and Mansehra — as, in his

75
00:02:48,184 --> 00:02:50,879
words, the areas "protected by Lashkar."

76
00:02:52,357 --> 00:02:54,779
Recruitment rarely starts with a weapon.

77
00:02:55,155 --> 00:02:56,561
It starts with a book.

78
00:02:57,737 --> 00:03:00,250
The first stage is called Daura-e-Sufa —

79
00:03:00,719 --> 00:03:01,214
roughly,

80
00:03:01,839 --> 00:03:03,388
"the course of purification."

81
00:03:03,868 --> 00:03:05,795
It runs about 21 days,

82
00:03:06,251 --> 00:03:07,579
based at Muridke.

83
00:03:08,011 --> 00:03:10,419
Headley described it in court as a "study

84
00:03:10,432 --> 00:03:10,966
course."

85
00:03:11,383 --> 00:03:13,323
Kasab, in his own confession,

86
00:03:13,831 --> 00:03:15,589
said it was where new arrivals were,

87
00:03:16,136 --> 00:03:16,917
in his words,

88
00:03:17,399 --> 00:03:19,391
indoctrinated in the tenets of jihad,

89
00:03:19,990 --> 00:03:22,177
along with recitation of the Quran and

90
00:03:22,177 --> 00:03:22,802
Hadith.

91
00:03:23,984 --> 00:03:25,169
There are no guns yet.

92
00:03:25,586 --> 00:03:28,476
This phase is about belief — establishing

93
00:03:28,489 --> 00:03:30,677
the ideological framework that everything

94
00:03:30,690 --> 00:03:32,396
else will be built on top of.

95
00:03:32,812 --> 00:03:34,857
Researchers who study radicalization

96
00:03:34,870 --> 00:03:36,823
pipelines note this is a deliberate

97
00:03:36,836 --> 00:03:39,023
sequencing choice common across militant

98
00:03:39,036 --> 00:03:39,544
groups:

99
00:03:40,156 --> 00:03:41,888
commitment is manufactured before

100
00:03:41,901 --> 00:03:42,656
capability.

101
00:03:44,017 --> 00:03:46,933
Once a recruit completes Sufa, he's moved

102
00:03:46,946 --> 00:03:49,472
to Muzaffarabad for Daura-e-Aam — the

103
00:03:49,668 --> 00:03:51,699
"general" or basic course.

104
00:03:52,078 --> 00:03:53,627
Another 21 days.

105
00:03:54,813 --> 00:03:56,519
According to the West Point Combating

106
00:03:56,532 --> 00:03:59,357
Terrorism Center's 2013 study of LeT

107
00:03:59,370 --> 00:03:59,904
fighters,

108
00:04:00,529 --> 00:04:02,365
this phase builds fundamental physical

109
00:04:02,378 --> 00:04:03,042
capacity —

110
00:04:03,589 --> 00:04:06,519
running, climbing, endurance — alongside

111
00:04:06,532 --> 00:04:08,225
continued exhortation toward what the

112
00:04:08,238 --> 00:04:09,201
group calls jihad.

113
00:04:09,618 --> 00:04:12,157
Other accounts describe early, basic

114
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:14,475
weapons familiarization beginning here as

115
00:04:14,488 --> 00:04:14,865
well.

116
00:04:16,044 --> 00:04:18,466
This is also, structurally, a filtering

117
00:04:18,479 --> 00:04:19,052
stage.

118
00:04:19,469 --> 00:04:21,513
Not everyone who starts a Daura-e-Aam

119
00:04:21,513 --> 00:04:22,958
course is judged fit —

120
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:26,292
physically or ideologically — to continue.

121
00:04:27,678 --> 00:04:29,866
Here's a detail most retellings of this

122
00:04:29,879 --> 00:04:32,509
story skip — because it isn't dramatic.

123
00:04:32,885 --> 00:04:33,627
It's boring.

124
00:04:34,006 --> 00:04:35,764
And that's exactly the point.

125
00:04:36,935 --> 00:04:39,149
Kasab testified that after his first two

126
00:04:39,162 --> 00:04:41,948
courses, he was told that before advancing

127
00:04:41,961 --> 00:04:44,110
to specialized training, he had to

128
00:04:44,123 --> 00:04:46,011
complete what's called khidmat — camp

129
00:04:46,024 --> 00:04:46,636
service.

130
00:04:47,013 --> 00:04:47,482
Chores.

131
00:04:47,899 --> 00:04:50,477
Roughly two months of it, by his account,

132
00:04:50,763 --> 00:04:52,964
before he was even allowed to go home and

133
00:04:52,977 --> 00:04:53,979
see his parents.

134
00:04:55,155 --> 00:04:56,666
Think about what that does

135
00:04:56,679 --> 00:04:57,785
organizationally.

136
00:04:58,202 --> 00:05:00,064
It's not written into any official

137
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:00,728
"curriculum,"

138
00:05:01,249 --> 00:05:03,332
but it functions as a loyalty test.

139
00:05:03,749 --> 00:05:06,001
Anyone who was in it purely for adventure,

140
00:05:06,561 --> 00:05:08,228
or who couldn't tolerate months of

141
00:05:08,241 --> 00:05:10,702
unglamorous labor with no timeline given,

142
00:05:11,275 --> 00:05:12,876
filters themselves out here.

143
00:05:13,293 --> 00:05:15,780
What's left is a smaller, more committed

144
00:05:15,793 --> 00:05:18,007
group headed into the advanced stage.

145
00:05:19,378 --> 00:05:21,644
This is the phase that turns a believer

146
00:05:21,657 --> 00:05:22,777
into an operative.

147
00:05:23,914 --> 00:05:27,247
Daura-e-Khaas — the "special" course —

148
00:05:27,547 --> 00:05:29,240
runs around three months.

149
00:05:29,658 --> 00:05:31,872
Multiple sources, including the Counter

150
00:05:31,885 --> 00:05:34,320
Extremism Project, describe it as

151
00:05:34,333 --> 00:05:36,468
specialty training in guerrilla warfare.

152
00:05:36,885 --> 00:05:38,760
Court records and researcher accounts

153
00:05:38,773 --> 00:05:40,869
reference weapons handling — assault

154
00:05:40,882 --> 00:05:43,083
rifles, light machine guns, and related

155
00:05:43,096 --> 00:05:45,635
ordnance — along with ambush tactics and

156
00:05:45,648 --> 00:05:46,650
survival skills.

157
00:05:47,826 --> 00:05:49,480
We're not going to walk through tactical

158
00:05:49,493 --> 00:05:51,706
specifics here — that's not the point of

159
00:05:51,719 --> 00:05:54,271
this video, and frankly, that information

160
00:05:54,284 --> 00:05:55,912
doesn't belong in a public video

161
00:05:55,925 --> 00:05:57,097
regardless of source.

162
00:05:57,501 --> 00:05:58,946
What matters for understanding the

163
00:05:58,959 --> 00:06:00,287
organization is this:

164
00:06:00,743 --> 00:06:02,878
by the time a recruit finishes Khaas,

165
00:06:03,204 --> 00:06:05,209
roughly five to six months have passed

166
00:06:05,222 --> 00:06:07,071
since he first walked into Muridke.

167
00:06:07,488 --> 00:06:09,519
Five to six months of escalating

168
00:06:09,532 --> 00:06:12,162
commitment, each stage gating the next.

169
00:06:13,557 --> 00:06:14,885
For a smaller subset,

170
00:06:15,419 --> 00:06:17,398
training doesn't stop at Khaas.

171
00:06:18,577 --> 00:06:20,465
Headley testified to a further stage

172
00:06:20,478 --> 00:06:23,486
called Daura-e-Ribat, held near Mansehra —

173
00:06:23,512 --> 00:06:25,843
and, above that, a leadership course,

174
00:06:26,012 --> 00:06:27,822
which he said was lectured personally by

175
00:06:27,835 --> 00:06:29,658
two of the organization's most senior

176
00:06:29,671 --> 00:06:30,205
figures:

177
00:06:30,947 --> 00:06:33,473
founder Hafiz Saeed and operations chief

178
00:06:33,473 --> 00:06:34,814
Zaki-ur-Rehman Lakhvi.

179
00:06:35,231 --> 00:06:37,340
Asked in court what was taught about jihad

180
00:06:37,353 --> 00:06:39,593
at that level, Headley said the framing

181
00:06:39,606 --> 00:06:41,351
was explicitly about fighting what the

182
00:06:41,364 --> 00:06:43,864
group called enemies of Islam — and, in

183
00:06:43,877 --> 00:06:45,869
the Kashmir context, assisting local

184
00:06:45,882 --> 00:06:47,562
militants against the Indian army.

185
00:06:48,749 --> 00:06:50,741
Separately — and this is a detail worth

186
00:06:50,754 --> 00:06:52,616
